sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch01-08> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Predicting a system's outcome by a mathematical shortcut is normally possible only for special systems with simple behavior; other systems are computationally irreducible, so their behavior can be found only by tracing each step with roughly as much computational effort as the system itself expends.";
    dc:type "general-principle";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Most systems with non-simple behavior are computationally irreducible";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 1, “The Foundations for a New Kind of Science”; section “An Outline of Basic Ideas” (from p. 1), p. 6. Scope: systems whose behavior is not obviously simple"
}
